1
00:00:00,194 --> 00:00:04,222
In mid-October, an R&D director spent 1908 yuan

2
00:00:04,272 --> 00:00:06,202
to renew his Kimi plan for a full year

3
00:00:06,352 --> 00:00:09,862
The next day, he asked Kimi to make a 5-minute video

4
00:00:09,942 --> 00:00:13,562
The task ran for 20-plus minutes and his quota fell from 100% to 0

5
00:00:13,622 --> 00:00:15,172
and the video never got made

6
00:00:15,411 --> 00:00:17,382
He asked customer service, and was told

7
00:00:17,432 --> 00:00:20,179
20-plus intermediate videos were generated along the way

8
00:00:20,229 --> 00:00:22,329
The quota was used up and can't be restored

9
00:00:22,559 --> 00:00:25,679
Anyone's first reaction would be: isn't this a rip-off

10
00:00:25,739 --> 00:00:29,163
They took a year's money, then cut you off after 20 minutes

11
00:00:29,213 --> 00:00:32,293
But that same month, the company sent out an apology letter

12
00:00:32,343 --> 00:00:34,303
and topped up every member's quota

13
00:00:34,354 --> 00:00:38,054
If it were just stinginess, why apologize while taking your money

14
00:00:38,214 --> 00:00:40,324
The catch is in that one extra use

15
00:00:40,386 --> 00:00:42,126
For you, it costs nothing

16
00:00:42,202 --> 00:00:44,842
But for the AI, every computation costs money

17
00:00:45,042 --> 00:00:46,342
This is Pixel Breath

18
00:00:46,392 --> 00:00:50,142
Today, let's talk about why AI memberships never seem to be enough

19
00:00:51,047 --> 00:00:54,047
First, where does the idea that they're stingy come from

20
00:00:54,397 --> 00:00:56,377
We've used the internet for decades

21
00:00:56,431 --> 00:00:59,128
and one more user costs almost nothing extra

22
00:00:59,178 --> 00:01:02,590
A textbook example: once an album is recorded

23
00:01:02,640 --> 00:01:05,500
pressing one more CD costs less than 1 dollar

24
00:01:05,610 --> 00:01:08,730
and one more download online costs almost nothing

25
00:01:09,060 --> 00:01:12,163
That's why apps dare to be free and promise unlimited use

26
00:01:12,213 --> 00:01:14,806
Giving you one more costs them almost nothing

27
00:01:14,856 --> 00:01:18,066
If something like that still runs out, that's truly stingy

28
00:01:18,326 --> 00:01:19,810
But AI is different

29
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:22,416
Every answer it gives really runs the GPUs

30
00:01:22,466 --> 00:01:23,736
to do the computation

31
00:01:23,886 --> 00:01:25,136
and burns real electricity

32
00:01:25,186 --> 00:01:28,416
Kimi's own top-up pack says: ask it to recommend a song

33
00:01:28,466 --> 00:01:30,052
and it costs about 3 cents

34
00:01:30,102 --> 00:01:33,282
Ask it to plan a week's road trip from Chengdu to west Sichuan

35
00:01:33,332 --> 00:01:34,812
and it costs about 1.6 yuan

36
00:01:34,872 --> 00:01:36,502
Put simply

37
00:01:36,552 --> 00:01:38,882
every extra answer has a price

38
00:01:38,953 --> 00:01:41,222
In economics, this is called marginal cost

39
00:01:41,272 --> 00:01:44,087
That is, the extra money it takes to make one more

40
00:01:44,137 --> 00:01:47,847
Episode 3 of 100 Principles of Economics covered marginal analysis

41
00:01:48,087 --> 00:01:50,460
This time, the margin falls on the seller's side

42
00:01:50,510 --> 00:01:54,070
But a monthly plan takes the price off your side, like a buffet

43
00:01:54,220 --> 00:01:57,440
Once you've paid, another plate doesn't cost you a cent

44
00:01:57,720 --> 00:02:00,655
So you keep taking until you're satisfied

45
00:02:00,705 --> 00:02:03,686
It's not just in China. In early November, OpenAI said

46
00:02:03,736 --> 00:02:07,419
from December, ChatGPT and Codex will share one quota

47
00:02:07,469 --> 00:02:08,829
counted in tokens

48
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:10,990
Elon Musk also said in a reply to a user

49
00:02:11,061 --> 00:02:13,721
that Grok Bot's serving costs relative to revenue

50
00:02:13,911 --> 00:02:15,231
are unsustainably high

51
00:02:15,292 --> 00:02:18,912
The way I see it, on the seller's side every reply has a price

52
00:02:19,082 --> 00:02:21,562
while on your side every reply costs zero

53
00:02:21,614 --> 00:02:25,304
The two sides don't add up, so what can rein you in

54
00:02:26,219 --> 00:02:27,483
With ordinary goods

55
00:02:27,533 --> 00:02:29,939
when more people want something, demand goes up

56
00:02:30,089 --> 00:02:32,459
and when demand goes up, prices rise

57
00:02:32,609 --> 00:02:34,573
Once it's too expensive for anyone

58
00:02:34,623 --> 00:02:36,319
it stops rising

59
00:02:36,385 --> 00:02:38,415
Price does the queuing for everyone

60
00:02:38,465 --> 00:02:40,485
When a monthly plan fixes the price

61
00:02:40,535 --> 00:02:42,315
other tools are all that's left

62
00:02:42,405 --> 00:02:45,127
like usage caps, queues, halting sales

63
00:02:45,177 --> 00:02:46,527
or giving you a little less

64
00:02:46,592 --> 00:02:48,422
In economics, this is called rationing

65
00:02:48,479 --> 00:02:52,549
Not price, but rules decide how much everyone gets

66
00:02:52,599 --> 00:02:55,820
Within a monthly quota, some people always use it really hard

67
00:02:55,870 --> 00:02:58,880
When Anthropic added limits for subscribers last year, it said

68
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:02,028
fewer than 5% of users would actually hit them

69
00:03:02,288 --> 00:03:04,138
But a few eat up the lion's share

70
00:03:04,188 --> 00:03:06,105
and most wait in line with them

71
00:03:06,155 --> 00:03:09,075
Over the past six months, Kimi has tried all of these

72
00:03:09,125 --> 00:03:11,070
each time right after a model launch

73
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:14,745
In April, as soon as a new model launched, members had to queue

74
00:03:14,995 --> 00:03:17,685
In July, just two days after a new model launched

75
00:03:17,737 --> 00:03:21,357
requests far exceeded forecasts, so they simply stopped selling memberships

76
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:25,212
In their words, to allocate current computing power to existing members

77
00:03:25,362 --> 00:03:28,382
In late October, two days after yet another model launched

78
00:03:28,433 --> 00:03:30,393
Kimi publicly apologized to everyone

79
00:03:30,443 --> 00:03:33,353
and gave all members back a quarter of a month's quota

80
00:03:33,406 --> 00:03:35,837
In the same letter, Kimi also said

81
00:03:35,887 --> 00:03:39,667
we're cutting unnecessary Agent calls and output length

82
00:03:39,807 --> 00:03:43,355
In plain words: with the same quota, the AI does a bit less

83
00:03:43,405 --> 00:03:45,112
and burns fewer unneeded tokens

84
00:03:45,162 --> 00:03:46,311
The way I see it

85
00:03:46,361 --> 00:03:49,409
writing those few lines less is also a form of rationing

86
00:03:49,459 --> 00:03:51,559
Textbooks on price controls say so too

87
00:03:51,614 --> 00:03:55,044
once prices are capped, quality may also go down

88
00:03:55,394 --> 00:03:57,878
Why does it always happen when a new model launches

89
00:03:57,928 --> 00:04:00,128
New models can do more and spend more

90
00:04:00,192 --> 00:04:02,132
Our director asked it for a video

91
00:04:02,182 --> 00:04:04,833
and it made 20 intermediate videos in one go

92
00:04:04,883 --> 00:04:07,233
That time in October, Kimi itself admitted

93
00:04:07,283 --> 00:04:09,510
part of it was unnecessary quota consumption

94
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:11,280
This isn't unique to AI either

95
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:14,622
At the end of 1996, America's AOL changed dial-up internet

96
00:04:14,657 --> 00:04:17,547
to 19.95 dollars a month, use as much as you like

97
00:04:17,667 --> 00:04:20,154
Daily time online rose from 14 minutes in September

98
00:04:20,204 --> 00:04:22,714
to 32 minutes by January the next year

99
00:04:23,104 --> 00:04:24,524
Dial-up lines were all busy

100
00:04:24,774 --> 00:04:27,024
The CEO publicly asked people to log on less

101
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:30,830
Later, more than 30 states forced it to refund users by time online

102
00:04:30,898 --> 00:04:33,768
When price isn't in play, someone has to wait in line

103
00:04:34,078 --> 00:04:35,398
And who's waiting in line

104
00:04:35,448 --> 00:04:36,688
You, the paying member

105
00:04:37,411 --> 00:04:40,481
Not every shortage means compute is really short

106
00:04:40,671 --> 00:04:41,841
Like in October

107
00:04:41,891 --> 00:04:45,171
Kimi itself said part of it was the AI spending too much

108
00:04:45,361 --> 00:04:47,741
And in April, they even deducted quota by mistake

109
00:04:47,931 --> 00:04:50,091
then restored everyone to 100% that night

110
00:04:50,391 --> 00:04:52,051
Wrong bills, unclear rules

111
00:04:52,101 --> 00:04:54,071
can also make you feel ripped off

112
00:04:54,331 --> 00:04:57,292
The other path is to let price do its job again

113
00:04:57,357 --> 00:04:59,107
Heavy users and peak-hour users

114
00:04:59,157 --> 00:05:01,217
should pay a bit more accordingly

115
00:05:01,477 --> 00:05:04,867
DeepSeek tried it last year: when people used it off-peak

116
00:05:04,937 --> 00:05:06,647
prices went as low as 75% off

117
00:05:06,837 --> 00:05:08,917
How to take that path is another story

118
00:05:09,217 --> 00:05:11,177
You've bought unlimited before

119
00:05:11,227 --> 00:05:12,827
In September 2019

120
00:05:12,877 --> 00:05:16,107
China Telecom stopped selling throttled unlimited plans

121
00:05:16,163 --> 00:05:18,804
the kind that slows down after a certain amount

122
00:05:18,854 --> 00:05:20,204
The plans were changed

123
00:05:20,254 --> 00:05:23,476
so data over the limit is charged by usage

124
00:05:23,526 --> 00:05:24,846
and ads could no longer say

125
00:05:24,896 --> 00:05:27,356
speeds get throttled after a certain amount of data

126
00:05:27,406 --> 00:05:29,656
So next time you see the word unlimited

127
00:05:29,706 --> 00:05:31,226
Please ask one more question

128
00:05:31,416 --> 00:05:34,171
Does giving me one more actually cost them anything

129
00:05:34,221 --> 00:05:37,631
If it costs almost nothing, unlimited might be real

130
00:05:37,821 --> 00:05:39,731
But if every single use costs money

131
00:05:39,781 --> 00:05:43,247
unlimited will sooner or later turn into caps, queues, or price hikes

132
00:05:43,297 --> 00:05:46,865
Back to the start: no one stole that director's quota

133
00:05:46,915 --> 00:05:49,644
It was spent because doing one more cost him nothing

134
00:05:49,694 --> 00:05:52,324
A membership has always sold a fixed amount of quota

135
00:05:52,376 --> 00:05:54,106
just not written in plain sight

136
00:05:54,286 --> 00:05:55,836
What I still can't figure out is

137
00:05:55,886 --> 00:05:57,754
if one day an extra AI answer

138
00:05:57,755 --> 00:05:59,676
got so cheap it cost almost nothing

139
00:05:59,756 --> 00:06:02,166
would unlimited plans come back
